-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- integers as differences of words
-- An integer is a pair of words (p , q) standing for p − q (Z2); Int is its normal form: toZ2 and
-- fromZ2 are inverse up to the relation p + q' = p' + q, and fromZ2 forgets a common summand
-- (fromZ2-shift). Addition and negation are the coordinatewise operations; their laws are the
-- words' (plusw-comm, plusw-assoc) transported through fromZ2. Order: p + q' ≤ p' + q (Lew is
-- lib/linproof.cub's; here as a Σ over a witness, the same type). The laws that need the words'
-- commutativity and associativity (plusw-comm, plusw-assoc are lib/linproof.cub's) are in
-- lib/intproof.cub.
Z2 : Type
Z2 = Word × Word
